nding up 12%, with a C$450M tender due in Q2 2027.\n- Both utilities' plans are mostly substations and transformers (docs `utility-capex-2027`, `utility-tenders`).\n- Pellham is the largest single buyer of grid equipment in Caldria.\n- VLTX holdings won 60% of the last tender round. Its quarterly utility order intake rose steadily from C$610M in 2025Q4 to C$670M in 2026Q3 (series `vltx_orders`).\n\n**Data centres (rising, but decelerating)**\n- Grid connection requests are expected to grow 9% in 2027, slower than in 2026. The report says power availability limits new sites (`datacentre-demand`).\n- The connection queue grew 4% in Q3 2026. Data centres are 60% of new requests, and the average wait is 26 months (`grid-queue`).\n- The long waits and power limits suggest that equipment demand is tied to grid capacity, so it may be capped.\n\n**For VLTX**\n- Utilities are 62% of VLTX holdings' orders. Pellham is 35% of those utility orders and Norvane is 21% (`vltx-order-mix`). That makes the two utilities' tender awards the main swing factor for VLTX.\n\n**Caveats**\n- These are planned figures, not awards. Tender timing and outcomes are not guaranteed, and VLTX could lose share.\n- Whether the utilities can fund their plans is a separate question. Colleague node n6 found that Pellham in particular cannot reliably fund its plan (n6.r1), and n5 found debt markets are only partly sufficient (n5.r1). A funding shortfall could delay or shrink the tenders.","ttft_ms":2236,"type":"result","duration_ms":53565,"uuid":"761f8b42-f873-4b16-9bca-21c674365187","ttft_stream_ms":1796,"time_to_request_ms":108,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":13,"system_prompt":13,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":9,"input_attachments":25,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":5,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":8,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":0,"loop_entry":5,"message_prep":0,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":6,"query_setup":0,"model_ga